The culprit is finding `crime_00008`, **Sync I/O in Hot Path**. Its evidence verbatim is:

> “`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  
> “`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  
> “`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  
> “lines: 12, 13, 15”  
> “swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it”

The blocking methods are `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`. Each blocks the event loop per request.

Smallest migration:

- Import `stat` and `readFile` from `node:fs/promises`.
- Remove `existsSync`; attempt the asynchronous operation and handle an `ENOENT` error instead. This also avoids an existence-check race.
- Replace `statSync` and `readFileSync` with awaited `stat` and `readFile`.
- Make only the internal `loadPlanSchema` helper async, then await it inside the handler.
- Keep the exported handler’s parameters and response body/status contract unchanged. If it is already async, its public signature need not change.

```ts
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

async function loadPlanSchema(path: string) {
  try {
    const [metadata, contents] = await Promise.all([
      stat(path),
      readFile(path, "utf8"),
    ]);

    // Preserve the existing validation and returned value.
    return { metadata, contents };
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      error instanceof Error &&
      "code" in error &&
      error.code === "ENOENT"
    ) {
      // Preserve the existing missing-file behavior.
      return null;
    }

    throw error;
  }
}

// Within the existing async request handler:
const schema = await loadPlanSchema(schemaPath);
```

If `stat` supplies no value used by the function, remove it too and just `await readFile(...)`. A truly synchronous exported return type cannot be preserved while moving filesystem work off the event loop; in that case, the narrowest necessary contract change is returning a `Promise` while preserving the resolved response shape.
